WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.018
Last year, American workplaces
recorded 2.5 million injuries.

2
00:00:05.018 --> 00:00:08.680
A lot of them happened
right in front of a camera.

3
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.950
And almost nobody was watching.

4
00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:14.233
This is Industrial Safety.

5
00:00:14.233 --> 00:00:19.300
An AI safety reviewer for
workplace camera footage.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.750
Here's the problem. Having a
safety specialist review footage

7
00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.554
costs about $57 an hour, per camera.

8
00:00:27.554 --> 00:00:33.161
So nobody does it, and footage only
gets pulled after someone gets hurt.

9
00:00:33.161 --> 00:00:37.600
Industrial Safety does that
first pass for $3.08 an hour.

10
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.895
It runs on your own computer.

11
00:00:40.895 --> 00:00:45.100
Clone the repo, run one
command, and you're in.

12
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.419
Pick a video, and it reviews
the footage as it plays.

13
00:00:50.419 --> 00:00:55.299
Every 10 seconds gets checked:
struck-by hazards, falls,

14
00:00:55.299 --> 00:01:00.440
fire, PPE, blocked exits,
unstable stacks, slips and trips.

15
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:07.150
Here, it flags the kitchen fire as a
priority within seconds of the flare-up.

16
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:11.191
Behind each review is
a team of AI agents.

17
00:01:11.191 --> 00:01:13.657
A coordinator splits the video.

18
00:01:13.657 --> 00:01:16.600
An observer fills a safety checklist.

19
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.180
A safety lead sends each
case to specialists,

20
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.600
and an independent
challenger re-checks the

21
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.100
original frames before
anything reaches you.

22
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:33.159
Every finding shows the exact frames
the AI looked at, with timestamps.

23
00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:37.008
It says what it can see,
and what it can't confirm.

24
00:01:37.008 --> 00:01:39.650
And a person always makes the call.

25
00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:46.600
Then it rolls everything into a one-page
report a manager can read in a minute.

26
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.988
I also tested what happens if someone

27
00:01:49.988 --> 00:01:53.112
writes instructions on
a sign in the video,

28
00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:55.945
telling the AI to mark everything safe.

29
00:01:55.945 --> 00:01:59.796
Even if the model obeys,
the code rejects the answer,

30
00:01:59.796 --> 00:02:04.300
and a skipped check is recorded
as not checked. Never as safe.

31
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.966
Running live, 24/7, that's
about $3 an hour per camera.

32
00:02:09.966 --> 00:02:12.314
18× cheaper than a person,

33
00:02:12.314 --> 00:02:17.100
with hard spending caps on
every run, day, and month.

34
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.458
It's not perfect yet.

35
00:02:19.458 --> 00:02:24.591
In my own timed test, reviewing
with the app was slower per clip,

36
00:02:24.591 --> 00:02:27.434
and it missed 2 of 3 fast accidents.

37
00:02:27.434 --> 00:02:29.250
Catching those is next.

38
00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:35.048
Industrial Safety. Coverage
nobody could afford before.

39
00:02:35.048 --> 00:02:37.550
It's open source on GitHub.
